== 5.1 Agents and Transmission

A *pathogen* is an organism or infectious agent that can cause disease. Infection occurs when an agent enters and multiplies in a host; infection does not always produce noticeable symptoms. A communicable disease can be transmitted directly or indirectly between hosts under suitable conditions. Different infections spread by different routes, so do not apply one disease's transmission route to every infection.

Examples include respiratory transmission through infectious particles in the air; ingestion of contaminated food or water; contact with infected body fluids; and transmission by vectors such as mosquitoes. The same disease may have more than one route, and not every contact causes infection. Appropriate prevention targets the relevant route and can include ventilation, safe water, hand hygiene, vaccination, safer practices and vector control.

