== 5.2 Malaria and Waterborne Illness

Malaria is caused by *Plasmodium* parasites. It is commonly transmitted through bites of infected female *Anopheles* mosquitoes; malaria is not caused by the mosquito itself. Fever can have several causes, so a fever alone does not confirm malaria. Prompt testing and appropriate treatment are important, especially for people at higher risk. Severe symptoms such as confusion, seizures or difficulty breathing require urgent care.

In areas at risk, use insecticide-treated nets as recommended and follow local guidance on vector control and other preventive measures. Vaccines and preventive medicines may be available for particular groups or settings; eligibility depends on current local programmes and individual advice. Protection involves more than one measure, and no single intervention guarantees zero risk.

Some diarrhoeal diseases spread through contaminated food or water. Safe water, hand hygiene, sanitation and appropriate food handling can reduce transmission. Diarrhoea can cause dehydration, especially in young children. Seek appropriate clinical advice; severe dehydration, inability to drink or other concerning symptoms require prompt care. Do not assume every diarrhoeal illness has the same cause.

#rule-box("Match cause and vector")[
  The malaria parasite is the cause; an infected female Anopheles mosquito is a common vector. A vector transmits an agent but is not the pathogen itself.
]

== 5.3 Tuberculosis and HIV

Tuberculosis (TB) is caused by bacteria and commonly affects the lungs. TB affecting the lungs can spread through the air when a person with infectious pulmonary TB releases infectious particles, especially in poorly ventilated settings. Persistent cough and other symptoms warrant assessment; symptoms alone cannot confirm TB. Testing and a complete treatment plan require a qualified health professional.

HIV is a virus that affects the immune system. It can be transmitted through certain body fluids, including during sexual contact, through contaminated blood or needles, and from a parent to a baby during pregnancy, birth or breastfeeding. It is not spread by ordinary contact such as hugging or sharing food. Testing and appropriate prevention and care are important. Antiretroviral therapy (ART) treats HIV but is not a cure; effective treatment supports health and can suppress the virus. A person with a sustained undetectable viral load does not sexually transmit HIV, according to WHO guidance.

Correct and consistent condom use reduces the risk of HIV and many other sexually transmitted infections and can help prevent pregnancy, but no method makes every risk disappear. Other prevention options and their suitability should be discussed with a qualified professional. Respect privacy and avoid stigma: the way an infection spreads does not justify discrimination.

#concept-box("Do not confuse the routes")[
  Malaria is commonly mosquito-borne, infectious pulmonary TB spreads through the air, and HIV transmission involves specified body-fluid exposures. Casual social contact is not an HIV transmission route.
]

== 5.4 Prevention, Vaccination and Care

Vaccines train the immune system to recognise particular infectious agents and reduce the risk of disease or severe outcomes. Protection depends on the vaccine, schedule, individual response and other factors. Follow current local immunisation advice; vaccination does not make hygiene, ventilation or appropriate care irrelevant.

Testing can help identify an infection and guide treatment. Antibiotics treat susceptible bacterial infections, not viruses; the appropriate medicine depends on the cause. Accurate information, access to care and respectful communication all support prevention and treatment. This chapter is educational and is not a substitute for diagnosis or emergency care.

== 5.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "The cause of malaria", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [Malaria is caused by \
A. Plasmodium parasites \
B. all mosquitoes by themselves \
C. a vitamin deficiency \
D. a bone fracture],
  [The disease is caused by Plasmodium parasites; an infected female Anopheles mosquito is a common vector. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "Clear water", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A community wants to reduce food- and waterborne infection. Which action is relevant? \
A. Assume clear water is always safe \
B. Improve safe water, sanitation and hand hygiene \
C. Use another person's prescription \
D. Ignore food handling],
  [Safe water, sanitation and hygiene address important transmission pathways. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "HIV and social contact", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [Which contact does not transmit HIV? \
A. Exposure to contaminated blood through a shared needle \
B. Certain sexual exposures \
C. Hugging and sharing food in ordinary social contact \
D. Transmission during pregnancy, birth or breastfeeding],
  [HIV is not spread by ordinary social contact such as hugging or sharing food. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "Symptoms and diagnosis", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [A person in a malaria-risk area has fever. What conclusion is most appropriate? \
A. Fever proves malaria in every case \
B. No assessment is ever needed \
C. Every fever is caused by the same virus \
D. Fever has several possible causes; seek timely assessment and appropriate testing],
  [Fever alone does not establish malaria. Timely assessment and testing help identify the cause and appropriate care. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Prevention is route-specific")[
  A mosquito net helps reduce mosquito bites; it does not make drinking water safe. Match the intervention to the disease and pathway.
]

